{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>The refining pot is for silver and the furnace for gold, and YHWH tests hearts (Proverbs 17:3).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, admonishes in this verse (Proverbs 17) concerning man, that he should purify his thoughts and rectify the traits of the heart, because the heart is the principal of all the organs in the body, and all the organs are drawn after it and subservient to it. It is in the middle of the body, ruling over them, directing them and infusing its power into all of them, and they are all its servants and attendants. Its image is like a king in the middle of the city, watching over all his surroundings and commanding the people of the city how to conduct themselves. And since the life of all the other organs depends on the heart, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He — who is the life of all existent beings altogether — is called by the term 'heart,' as it is written (Psalms 73): ״צור לבבי וחלקי אלהים לעולם״, and just as the activities of the heart are to direct and watch over all the organs in man, so the Holy One, blessed be He, directs all existent beings altogether and watches over all the corners, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 8): ״כי אני ה' בקרב הארץ״. The essence of the Torah in its entirety depends on the heart. And thus the Torah is sealed in the heart, from Genesis until לעיני כל ישראל, taken from the Wisdom in which there are 32 wondrous paths of wisdom. And since all of a man's actions depend on the heart, therefore Solomon admonished that a man should be careful with the traits of the heart, and he informed us that all the thoughts hidden in the heart are revealed before HaShem, may He be exalted, for He knows thoughts and examines hearts — which is a good heart and which is an evil heart. And this is what the verse says: מצרף לכסף וכור לזהב ובוחן לבות ה׳ — He says: just as the refiner examines silver in the refining pot and gold in the furnace (which is a vessel made by a potter for refining gold), and sees the silver — whether it is silver of dross or choice silver — and likewise the gold, so HaShem, may He be exalted, examines hearts — whether one is whole or lacking. From this we learn that the whole heart and the good thought are likened to refined silver, while the deficient heart and the evil thought are likened to silver of dross. The good heart will ultimately come to good deed, just as choice silver ultimately comes to good craftwork; and the evil heart will ultimately come to evil deed, just as silver of dross is unfit for craftwork.\n\nIt may further include in ״ובוחן לבות״ that it speaks of the great day of judgment at the resurrection of the dead, and speaks of the intermediate ones — those who are half sins and half merits. And if among those sins there is not included the sin of the wicked of Israel in their bodies, their law is as complete righteous ones, as we have learned, according to Beth Hillel: ״ורב חסד מטה כלפי חסד״. But if among those sins there is included the sin of the wicked of Israel in their bodies, there is no escape for them from Gehenna, even according to Beth Hillel. And this is the refining spoken of here: מצרף לכסף וכור לזהב — that the Holy One, blessed be He, will refine them and examine them, in the manner of what is written (Zechariah 13): ״והבאתי את השלישית באש וצרפתים כצרוף את הכסף ובנתים כבחון את הזהב״. And the word כור testifies to the fire, and Gehenna — which is the place of judgment — is likened to the כור, which is the vessel of smelting. And this is מצרף לכסף וכור לזהב — and for whom? For the intermediate ones in whose sins is included the sin of the wicked of Israel in their bodies. And this is ״בוחן לבות ה׳״, for the expression בחינה applies only to the intermediate ones, for the complete righteous and the complete wicked have no need of examination, since their judgment is already decided and sealed — these for the life of the World to Come and those for Gehenna, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 12): ״אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם״. But the intermediate ones, those who are balanced — they are the ones who need examination: whether they will merit and be among the class of the righteous, or will not merit and be among the class of the wicked. This requires the examination of HaShem, blessed be He, according to the standard of His knowledge, may He be exalted.\n\nIt may further include in ״ובוחן לבות״ that He, blessed be He, brings them to examination and trial, for besides the fact that He examines them and knows them for Himself, He further examines them for others — that is, He brings them to examination in order to make known the merit of their heart to creatures, in the manner of what is written: ״וצרפתים כצרוף את הכסף ובחנתים כבחון את הזהב״. And it is known that the Holy One, blessed be He, examines none but the righteous, as it is written (Psalms 11): ״ה' צדיק יבחן״ — a parable: to a smelter who sells vessels — he does not test the faulty ones among them but the strong ones. Examination and trial is not needed by the Holy One, blessed be He, for the thought hidden in the heart is revealed before Him; therefore it says ״ובוחן לבות ה׳״. And thus Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 15): ״שאול ואבדון נגד ה' אף כי לבות בני אדם״ — the meaning of the verse: if what is hidden in the depths of the earth is revealed before Him, blessed be He, and is known to Him and examined by Him — how much more so the heart, which is the dwelling place of the soul of wisdom which He bestowed from His holy spirit, which is revealed before Him and examined by Him. But the need for this is for the creatures of the world, who do not know the hidden but only the revealed and what is seen by the eyes, in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 16): ״כי האדם יראה לעינים וה' יראה ללבב״. And when He examines the righteous and his pure thought and his clean heart emerge from potentiality into actuality, then the greatness of the obligation of service becomes known and the name of Heaven is sanctified in the world — in the manner of the trial of Abraham, about whom it is written (Genesis 22): ״והאלהים נסה את אברהם״, and the trial was only to make known the obligation of service to the creatures. And this is what HaShem, may He be exalted, said to him (ibid.): ״כי עתה ידעתי כי ירא אלהים אתה״ — its meaning: it is now known to all that you are one who fears Elohim.\n\nAnd likewise the trial of Israel, when they went out of Egypt — most of the miracles that were performed for them in the wilderness were in order to examine their heart and bring them to trial. In the manner of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds: the sea was not split for them all at once to make for them a long path within it from beginning to end, but little by little — for the sea would flee before them, and as they passed they would see the sea before their eyes; and so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 114): ״הים ראה וינוס״. And likewise the descent of the manna: it did not descend for them all at once for a month or two months, but ״דבר יום ביומו״ — each day's portion in its day. And why was it necessary to do so? In order to accustom their nature to the level of trust (bitachon), and so that their eyes would be dependent each and every day upon their Father in Heaven, and in order to examine their heart to see whether they would harbor doubts about the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He. And so too, when Pharaoh sent Israel, HaShem, may He be exalted, led them by way of the wilderness — and all was to test them and examine their heart, when they went out with their wives and children into that great and awesome wilderness of serpent, viper, and scorpion. And it was possible to lead them by the near way, which is the land of the Philistines, but He did not do so. And this is what is written:" "<b>And it came to pass, when Pharaoh let the people go, that Elohim did not lead them by the way of the land of the Philistines, because it was near; for Elohim said: Lest the people reconsider when they see war, and return to Egypt. Lest the people reconsider.</b> There is no doubt on the part of the Holy One, blessed be He, for He knew that the people would reconsider if He led them by the near way. Rather, the meaning of פן ינחם is 'so that they would not reconsider'; and so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: Everywhere it says פן or אל it means nothing but 'you shall not do' (i.e., it constitutes a negative). And like it (Genesis 3): ״ועתה פן ישלח ידו״ — its meaning is 'so that he not stretch out his hand.' That is, it is not fitting for Me to give him the opportunity to take from the Tree of Life and eat of it and thereby nullify My decree. And likewise here: He led them by way of the wilderness and not by way of the land of the Philistines, in order not to give them the opportunity to return to Egypt by that near way." "<b>When they see war.</b> And even though they saw war on the way of the wilderness — namely, the war of Amalek — the war of Amalek was not one that would warrant their returning to Egypt because of it. Moreover, Israel did not pass through their territory; rather, Amalek came from afar, trespassing against the signs and wonders performed for Israel — for they had already heard of them and wanted to come upon them to fight against them, as those who prevail against the Holy One, blessed be He. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: If when they went by a roundabout way they said (Numbers 14) ״נתנה ראש ונשובה מצרימה״ — had He led them by a direct way, how much more so!" "<b>And</b> our Rabbi Hananel, of blessed memory, <b>wrote</b> that He led them by way of the wilderness for another reason: to multiply for them signs and wonders. For had He led them by the near way of the land of the Philistines, and the Holy One, blessed be He, placed it in their hearts to grant them permission to pass through their land and not hinder them at all, the sign would have been slight. Therefore wisdom required that He divert them by way of the wilderness, so that the signs would be many and great — in the descent of the manna and the coming of the quail and the bringing forth of water from the rock. For according to how far they were removed from civilization, the sign and wonder that would stand for the human species in those places would be greater. And such is the way of HaShem, blessed be He, in performing signs and wonders with the righteous: He performs for them a miracle within a miracle, to magnify the sign and the wonder. For so we find with Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah: when they were cast into the fiery furnace, it was possible with a slight sign to extinguish the fire, and to magnify the sign and wonder the fire was at the height of its power and did not harm them, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 3:27): ״די לא שלט נורא בגשמיהון ושער ראשהון לא התחרך וריח נור לא עדת בהון״. Also Daniel: it was possible with a slight sign that the Holy One, blessed be He, would kill the lions, and to magnify the sign He left them alive and they did not harm him, as it is written (ibid. 6): ״אלהי שלח מלאכיה וסגר פום אריותא ולא חבלוני״. Also Gideon, who came to fight with Midian: the camp of Midian was like the sand upon the seashore, and it was possible to perform a sign that he would defeat them with ten thousand men or with half of that, and a great sign would have sufficed. But in order to magnify the sign, he defeated them with three hundred men, for so it is written (Judges 7:2): ״רב העם אשר אתך מתתי את מדין בידם פן יתפאר עלי ישראל לאמר ידי הושיעה לי״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ועתה קרא נא באזני העם לאמר מי ירא וחרד ישוב ויצפור מהר הגלעד וישב מן העם עשרים ושנים אלף ועשרת אלפים נשארו״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ויאמר ה' אל גדעון עוד העם רב הורד אותם אל המים ואצרפנו לך שם״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ויאמר ה' אל גדעון בשלש מאות האיש המלקקים אושיע אתכם ונתתי את מדין בידך״." "<b>And know</b> that all the affairs of Israel and their encounters in the wilderness were all a pure trial, so that their rational soul would grow in the degrees of trust (bitachon), which is the root of faith, so that they would be worthy to receive the Torah. And for this reason He split the sea for them when they passed into it, and not all at once. Also after they came out of the Sea of Reeds to the wilderness of Shur and arrived at Marah — the waters were sweet, then turned bitter, and by means of the wood they returned to their sweetness — and all this was a pure trial, as it is written (Exodus 15): ״שם נסהו״. Also the descent of the manna, a day's portion in its day and not for many days — all a pure trial, as it is written (ibid. 16): ״ולקטו דבר יום ביומו למען אנסנו״. Also after they were in Rephidim and their hands grew slack from the Torah they had received at Marah — and as punishment for this Amalek came and fought against them, for through the sin of neglecting Torah troubles come to the world — all these affairs were a pure trial, in order to establish in their souls the trait of trust. And concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 22:19): ״להיות בה׳ מבטחך הודעתיך היום אף אתה״ — he says: what I have made known to you until this day in this book, of the musars and the parables, was all for your benefit and to bring you to the perfection of the trait of trust, just as I have attained it; this is what he says ״אף אתה״. He makes clear to us that the trait of trust is a great principle and the foundation of Torah and commandment." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ויהי בשלח פרעה — the mouth that said ״גם את ישראל לא אשלח״ is the one that said ״אנכי אשלח אתכם ואת טפכם״, and what was his wage? ״לא תתעב מצרי״. The mouth that said ״לא ידעתי את ה׳״ turned and said ״ה׳ הצדיק ואני ועמי הרשעים״, and what was his wage? He gave them burial, as it is written (Exodus 15:12): ״נטית ימינך תבלעמו ארץ״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:30.124440507Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}